**Reused evidence**
E0001 is the key attribution source here: Bolsonaro had federal executive authority in 2022, so social-policy choices and failures in that year are attributable to his administration within the limits of Brazil’s federal system.
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**New evidence**
Emergency-transfer expansion under EC 123.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Emenda Constitucional nº 123, de 14 de julho de 2022","publisher":"Presidência da República / Planalto","publication_date":"2022-07-14","url":"https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/constituicao/emendas/emc/emc123.htm","claim":"The 2022 constitutional amendment recognized a fuel-price emergency and expanded Auxílio Brasil, Auxílio Gás, trucker aid, and public-transport support through 31 December 2022.","locator":"art. 3 and art. 4; preamble","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text promulgated in 2022; direct legal record of federal welfare expansion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target year; the emergency and transfer measures were operative in Bolsonaro's final months.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Bolsonaro's federal administration, but Congress promulgated the amendment and the trigger was an external price shock, so attribution is partial rather than purely personal.","contrary_evidence":["The measure was emergency relief, not durable social reform.","Implementation and targeting still depended on bureaucracy and municipalities."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.6","6B.7","6B.10"]}

Auxílio Brasil reached 17.5 million families at the start of 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Programa Auxílio Brasil alcança patamar histórico no país","publisher":"Ministério da Cidadania","publication_date":"2022-01-19","url":"https://www.gov.br/pt-br/noticias/assistencia-social/2022/01/governo-federal-comeca-a-pagar-auxilio-brasil-a-17-5-milhoes-de-familias/","claim":"In January 2022, Auxílio Brasil reached 17.5 million families and the waiting list was zeroed, with total spending above R$7.1 billion.","locator":"headline and lede","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry release with concrete program coverage and expenditure figures for 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Bolsonaro federal administration, though take-up and local delivery were shared with the CadÚnico/SUAS machinery.","contrary_evidence":["Coverage alone does not prove adequacy, fairness, or durability.","The release does not show who was newly poor or whether targeting was clean."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.10"]}

Auxílio Brasil expanded again by November 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Auxílio Brasil chega a 21,53 milhões de famílias em novembro de 2022","publisher":"Ministério da Cidadania","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.gov.br/pt-br/noticias/assistencia-social/2022/11/auxilio-brasil-chega-a-21-53-milhoes-de-familias-em-novembro-de-2022","claim":"By November 2022 Auxílio Brasil served 21.53 million families, with an investment of R$13 billion and an average payment of R$607.57 per family.","locator":"headline and lede","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry release with a concrete 2022 coverage and payment snapshot.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Bolsonaro federal administration, but still mediated by registry rules and local administration.","contrary_evidence":["A larger transfer program does not by itself show improved living standards or clean targeting.","The election-year timing creates a plausible political-incentive concern."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.10"]}

CadÚnico modernization and targeting.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"D11016","publisher":"Presidência da República / Planalto","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2019-2022/2022/decreto/d11016.htm","claim":"The decree defines CadÚnico as the federal instrument for identifying and characterizing low-income families and for accessing and integrating social programs.","locator":"art. 1-3","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary legal text issued by the Bolsonaro presidency, specifying program architecture and intended social-function use.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro administration action: he promulgated the decree and it shaped the social-program machinery under his government.","contrary_evidence":["The decree shows targeting infrastructure, not actual delivery quality.","Better registry design does not guarantee fewer exclusions or fewer wrongful inclusions."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}

Pregnant-beneficiary identification in Auxílio Brasil.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"INSTRUÇÃO NORMATIVA CONJUNTA Nº 1, DE 4 DE MARÇO DE 2022","publisher":"Ministério do Desenvolvimento e Assistência Social, Família e Combate à Fome","publication_date":"2022-03-11","url":"https://www.gov.br/mds/pt-br/acesso-a-informacao/legislacao/instrucoes/instrucao-normativa-conjunta-no-1-de-4-de-marco-de-2022","claim":"The instruction established procedures to identify pregnant beneficiaries eligible for the Auxílio Brasil gestante benefit.","locator":"summary heading and opening text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official rule text describing a targeted benefit for a vulnerable group in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct federal-program design under Bolsonaro, though operational identification depended on local health and social-assistance networks.","contrary_evidence":["This is a targeting rule, not a demonstrated outcome.","No delivery or child-welfare impact is shown in the instruction itself."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.7"]}

Maternal-child primary care expansion.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ministério da Saúde lança Cuida Mais Brasil para fortalecer atendimento materno-infantil no SUS","publisher":"Ministério da Saúde","publication_date":"2022-01-06","url":"https://www.gov.br/saude/pt-br/assuntos/noticias/2022/janeiro/ministerio-da-saude-lanca-cuida-mais-brasil-para-fortalecer-atendimento-materno-infantil-no-sus/","claim":"The ministry launched Cuida Mais Brasil and said the first-year allocation would be R$194 million to add pediatricians and gynecologic-obstetric doctors to primary care teams.","locator":"lede and first paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official health-ministry release with explicit funding and staffing targets for a 2022 program.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro administration action through the federal health ministry, but implementation still depended on local SUS capacity.","contrary_evidence":["Announced staffing does not prove full nationwide coverage or sustained quality.","The program's effect on outcomes is not demonstrated in the launch notice."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9"]}

Child primary-care access and quality.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IBGE surveys how parents and responsible persons address child Primary Healthcare for the first time ever","publisher":"IBGE","publication_date":"2022-12-21","url":"https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-news/2184-agency-news/news/35926-ibge-surveys-how-parents-and-responsible-persons-address-child-primary-healthcare-for-the-first-time-ever","claim":"Of 38 million children under 13 in Brazil in 2022, 82.9% were attended by any primary-healthcare service, but the general score for child primary healthcare was 5.7, below the minimum quality level of 6.6.","locator":"Highlights","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National statistical agency survey with explicit access and quality measures for 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"This is national-system performance under Bolsonaro's administration, but the result is only partially attributable to the president because SUS delivery is shared with states and municipalities.","contrary_evidence":["This is an access-and-quality snapshot, not direct proof of presidential competence.","Some service variation is subnational rather than federal."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.10"]}

Routine childhood immunization weakness.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No Dia Nacional da Vacinação, Ministério da Saúde reforça importância de manter o Brasil livre de doenças já eliminadas","publisher":"Ministério da Saúde","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://www.gov.br/saude/pt-br/assuntos/noticias/2022/outubro/no-dia-nacional-da-vacinacao-ministerio-da-saude-reforca-importancia-de-manter-o-brasil-livre-de-doencas-ja-eliminadas/","claim":"By 14 October 2022, polio vaccination coverage for children under 1 was 44.8%, and the national polio campaign had reached 65% of the target public.","locator":"second and third paragraphs after the lede","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official health-ministry reporting with an explicit 2022 coverage figure and campaign uptake.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Federal campaign coordination is attributable to the Bolsonaro administration, but the low coverage also reflects state/municipal delivery and vaccine hesitancy.","contrary_evidence":["Coverage is a shared-system outcome, so the president is not the only actor.","Campaign reach does not by itself show equitable or sustained coverage improvement."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}

COVID vaccination logistics and scale.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Governo Federal investiu R$ 38 bilhões em vacinas Covid-19 e criou logística inédita, permitindo alta cobertura vacinal dos brasileiros","publisher":"Ministério da Saúde","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.gov.br/saude/pt-br/assuntos/noticias/2022/dezembro/governo-federal-investiu-r-38-bilhoes-em-vacinas-covid-19-e-criou-logistica-inedita-permitindo-alta-cobertura-vacinal-dos-brasileiros/","claim":"By December 2022 the ministry said more than 568 million COVID-19 doses had been distributed and that R$38 billion had been invested in vaccination logistics and procurement.","locator":"headline and opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry record of federal crisis-response logistics and spending in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Bolsonaro federal government, although vaccine delivery itself was shared with states and municipalities.","contrary_evidence":["Large-scale distribution does not prove efficient management of all health services.","The release is self-evaluative and should be balanced against independent indicators of routine vaccine coverage."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6"]}

Targeting vulnerable traditional and specific groups.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Cadastro Único ultrapassa a marca de 3,76 milhões de famílias identificadas como Grupos Populacionais Tradicionais e Específicos","publisher":"Ministério da Cidadania","publication_date":"2022-05-24","url":"https://www.gov.br/pt-br/noticias/assistencia-social/2022/05/cadastro-unico-ultrapassa-a-marca-de-3-76-milhoes-de-familias-identificadas-como-grupos-populacionais-tradicionais-e-especificos","claim":"In April 2022 CadÚnico recorded more than 3.76 million families in traditional and specific groups, including Indigenous and quilombola households, and said these groups required differentiated registration to be prioritized by municipalities.","locator":"opening paragraphs and 'Mobilização e crescimento' section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry release with explicit vulnerable-group counts and targeting language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects Bolsonaro-era federal registration and outreach policy, though actual service access still depended heavily on local governments.","contrary_evidence":["The page demonstrates registry prioritization more than real-world service improvements.","It does not show whether those groups actually received adequate benefits or services."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}

Income recovery in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In 2022, labor market and Auxílio Brasil cash transfer program allow income recovery","publisher":"IBGE","publication_date":"2023-05-11","url":"https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-news/2184-agency-news/news/36873-em-2022-mercado-de-trabalho-e-auxilio-brasil-permitem-recuperacao-dos-rendimentos-3","claim":"IBGE reported that average real monthly household income per capita reached R$1,586 in 2022, up 6.9% from 2021, and explicitly linked the recovery to the labor market and Auxílio Brasil.","locator":"Highlights","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National statistics agency release using 2022 PNAD data and naming Auxílio Brasil in the explanation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective release about 2022 conditions; good fit for end-of-period outcome but not contemporaneous policy.","ruler_attribution":"Partial attribution only: IBGE itself splits the recovery between labor-market conditions and Auxílio Brasil, so Bolsonaro cannot be credited exclusively.","contrary_evidence":["The improvement was not solely a policy effect.","Income gains do not erase persistent deprivation or regional inequality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}

Poverty remained very high at the end of 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In 2023, poverty in the country drops to lowest level since 2012","publisher":"IBGE","publication_date":"2024-12-04","url":"https://agenciadenoticias.ibge.gov.br/en/agencia-news/2184-agency-news/news/42113-in-2023-poverty-in-the-country-drops-to-lowest-level-since-2012","claim":"IBGE reported that from 2022 to 2023 poverty fell from 31.6% to 27.4% and extreme poverty fell from 5.9% to 4.4%, establishing the 2022 end-of-term baseline as still very high.","locator":"Highlights","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National statistics agency release with explicit 2022 baseline figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022-2023; suitable for end-of-period baseline, not direct 2022 policy attribution.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual only: this is an outcome measure shaped by pandemic aftermath, labor markets, and later policy as well as Bolsonaro-era conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The release does not isolate Bolsonaro's contribution to the 2022 baseline.","It is retrospective and partly reflects dynamics after he left office."],"lenses":["6B.8","6B.10"]}

Child poverty was multidimensional and broad.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Há 32 milhões de crianças e adolescentes na pobreza no Brasil, alerta UNICEF","publisher":"UNICEF Brasil","publication_date":"2023-02-14","url":"https://www.unicef.org/brazil/innocenti/brazil/comunicados-de-imprensa/ha-32-milhoes-de-criancas-e-adolescentes-na-pobreza-no-brasil-alerta-unicef","claim":"UNICEF reported that 32 million Brazilian children and adolescents lived in multidimensional poverty, with deprivations in income, education, work, housing, water, sanitation, and information.","locator":"opening summary and paragraph beginning 'No Brasil, ao menos 32 milhões...'","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF is a highly credible international agency, and the release uses official Brazilian survey series through 2022/2021 data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mostly retrospective but anchored in Brazilian survey data through 2022; good for end-of-period social-condition context.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual: the report reflects structural deprivation and pandemic-era deterioration, with only partial attribution to Bolsonaro's federal policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["The report spans multiple years and is not a direct measure of one ruler-year.","Structural poverty in a large federative state cannot be assigned to the president alone."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

Education exclusion remained severe.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Dois milhões de crianças e adolescentes de 11 a 19 anos não estão frequentando a escola no Brasil, alerta UNICEF","publisher":"UNICEF Brasil","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://www.unicef.org/brazil/innocenti/brazil/comunicados-de-imprensa/dois-milhoes-de-criancas-e-adolescentes-de-11-a-19-anos-nao-estao-frequentando-a-escola-no-brasil","claim":"UNICEF and Ipec reported that 2 million children and adolescents aged 11-19 were not attending school in Brazil in 2022, about 11% of the sample.","locator":"headline and opening paragraph","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF release based on a specific 2022 survey sample, with clear quantitative findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual and only partially attributable to Bolsonaro, because schooling depends heavily on subnational governments and post-pandemic effects.","contrary_evidence":["The survey measures exclusion, not federal policy causation.","Subnational education management limits direct presidential attribution."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

Water access remained uneven and discriminatory.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2,1 milhões de crianças e adolescentes vivem sem acesso adequado a água no Brasil, alerta UNICEF","publisher":"UNICEF Brasil","publication_date":"2024-03-21","url":"https://www.unicef.org/brazil/innocenti/brazil/comunicados-de-imprensa/21-milhoes-de-criancas-e-adolescentes-vivem-sem-acesso-adequado-agua-no","claim":"Using the 2022 census, UNICEF said 2.1 million Brazilian children and adolescents lacked adequate access to drinking water, with disproportionate impacts on Black, Indigenous, North, and Northeast populations.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted statement from Rodrigo Resende","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Credible UN-agency analysis based on the 2022 census, but published later and therefore retrospective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2024 publication using 2022 census data; strong fit for end-of-period conditions, weaker for direct contemporaneous attribution.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual: water and sanitation are heavily subnational, though federal funding and priority-setting under Bolsonaro mattered indirectly.","contrary_evidence":["The report is based on census conditions, not a direct audit of Bolsonaro's decisions.","Sanitation and water provision are shared across federal, state, and municipal levels."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

Food insecurity worsened sharply in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2º Inquérito Nacional sobre Insegurança Alimentar no Contexto da Pandemia da Covid-19 no Brasil","publisher":"Rede Brasileira de Pesquisa em Soberania e SAN","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://redepenssan.org.br/2o-inquerito-nacional-sobre-inseguranca-alimentar-no-contexto-da-pandemia-da-covid-19-no-brasil/","claim":"The II VIGISAN found that 33.1 million people were in hunger in 2022 and that 58.7% of the population experienced some level of food insecurity, with worse outcomes in rural households and among Black and female-headed households.","locator":"opening summary and the sections on rural households and race/gender","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Large national survey by a respected research network, though not an official government statistic and still partly pandemic-period data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly centered on the 2021-2022 fieldwork window and published in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual and only partially attributable to Bolsonaro because the result reflects pandemic, inflation, labor-market, and policy factors together.","contrary_evidence":["The survey covers overlapping pandemic and recovery conditions, so causal attribution to Bolsonaro alone is not justified.","It measures household insecurity, not a direct program audit."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

CGU audit found weak CadÚnico controls and election-year distortions.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CGU avalia os procedimentos de inclusão e de averiguação cadastral de famílias unipessoais no CadÚnico","publisher":"Controladoria-Geral da União","publication_date":"2024-04-08","url":"https://www.gov.br/cgu/pt-br/assuntos/noticias/2024/04/cgu-avalia-os-procedimentos-de-inclusao-e-de-averiguacao-cadastral-de-familias-unipessoais-no-cadunico","claim":"CGU found that many CadÚnico 'families unipessoais' were likely not truly single-person households and noted that 15% of unipersonal families in August 2023 arose from split households, with the biggest movement occurring in July 2022 when Auxílio Brasil beneficiaries began receiving R$600 regardless of composition.","locator":"'Providências' and findings paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official anti-corruption/audit body reporting with concrete control-failure findings tied to the 2022 benefit structure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective audit of 2022 program conditions; strong for control and targeting assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Bolsonaro-era CadÚnico/Auxílio Brasil controls, though the audit is retrospective and does not by itself prove intentional vote-buying.","contrary_evidence":["This shows weak controls and possible opportunism, but not definitive proof of intentional personalist welfare manipulation.","Some problematic records may reflect administrative capacity failures rather than deliberate abuse."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across official Brazilian legal texts and ministries, IBGE releases, UNICEF/UN and NGO reports, CGU audits, and a few secondary summaries. I retained the best non-duplicative evidence above and rejected or downgraded several classes of material: generic WHO country pages that duplicated the local priors without adding ruler-specific 2022 detail; broad OECD/UNICEF education or climate pages that were too indirect; INEP Censo Escolar portal pages that were procedural rather than outcome-bearing in the snippets I found; and later 2023-2026 MDS pages about Lula-era Bolsa Família reforms that were out of period for Bolsonaro 2022. I also avoided repeating the same social-transfer fact through multiple publishers unless the source added a materially different claim, such as the CGU audit’s control-failure finding or the UNICEF/IBGE inequality breakdowns.

**Remaining gaps**
The biggest remaining gap is direct attribution. Brazil’s health, education, water, and sanitation delivery is heavily shared with states and municipalities, so many outcome measures are strong evidence of the 2022 social condition but only partial evidence of Bolsonaro’s personal contribution. More search would help on federal budget execution, program audits, and service-delivery evaluations for health, school meals, and sanitation if a later score were required. Evidence on durable institution-building beyond CadÚnico modernization and the existing SUS/PNI architecture is also thin.
